    {
      "block_id": 9,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "Figure 6-1: How Images are Stored in Memory\n",
      "bbox": [
        193.82896423339844,
        376.0090637207031,
        399.81787109375,
        386.5090637207031
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.3226019984357983,
        0.48713408993900437,
        0.6654425706463399,
        0.5007372406017737
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 10,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The map in Figure 6-1 represents a single bit-plane (one bit of color) of an image at word \naddresses 20 through 61. Each of these addresses accesses one word (16 pixels) of a single bit- \nplane. If this image required sixteen colors, four bit-planes like this would be required in \nmemory, and four copy (move) operations would be required to completely move the image.\n",
      "bbox": [
        82.92896270751953,
        414.6550598144531,
        514.1744384765625,
        464.153076171875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.13802400071353116,
        0.5372014525462496,
        0.8557735530042212,
        0.6013280215003667
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 11,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The blitter is very efficient at copying such blocks because it needs to be told only the starting \n",
      "bbox": [
        82.72897338867188,
        479.45208740234375,
        513.9849853515625,
        489.95208740234375
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.13769114564111598,
        0.6211484743346015,
        0.8554582339961635,
        0.6347516249973708
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 12,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "address (20), the destination address, and the size of the block (height = 6, width = 7). It will then \nautomatically move the data, one word at a time, whenever the data bus is available. When the \ntransfer is complete, the blitter will signal the processor with a flag and an interrupt.\n",
      "bbox": [
        82.9289321899414,
        492.3981018066406,
        514.2021484375,
        528.3461303710938
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.13802394992116518,
        0.6379205299940331,
        0.8558196724725389,
        0.6844925727170608
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 13,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "NOTE\n",
      "bbox": [
        281.4289245605469,
        543.4951171875,
        311.53240966796875,
        553.9951171875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.4684002405932792,
        0.7041186631981922,
        0.5185034049678388,
        0.7177218138609615
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 14,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "This copy (move) operation operates on memory and may or may not change the \nmemory currently being used for display.\n",
      "bbox": [
        106.92892456054688,
        562.4951171875,
        489.25482177734375,
        586.2951049804688
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.17796867919226728,
        0.7287338882070128,
        0.8142982338783252,
        0.7595676805612818
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 15,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "All data copy blits are performed as rectangles of words, with a given width and height All four \nDMA channels use a single blit size register, called BLTSIZE, used for both the width and height \nThe width can take a value of from 1 to 64 words (16 to 1024 bits). The height can run from 1 to \n",
      "bbox": [
        83.17892456054688,
        601.5941162109375,
        514.7374877929688,
        637.64111328125
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.13844002828525276,
        0.7793881333955166,
        0.8567106721567119,
        0.8260883935943608
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 16,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "1024 rows. The width is stored in the least significant six bits of the BLTSIZE register. If a \nvalue of zero is stored, a width count of 64 words is used. This is the only parameter in the blitter\n",
      "bbox": [
        83.4288330078125,
        639.9910888671875,
        514.7212524414062,
        663.589111328125
      ],
